{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ps52mAFBzpUtpwgHCLEnz4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Reframe entirely as diagnostic framework paper. Lead: \"We built\n2\t  ContamCal to calibrate contaminated benchmarks — and discovered why this is harder\n3\t  than expected.\" Three diagnostic contributions survive intact regardless of calibration\n4\t  failure: (1) Min-K++ directional reversal is a standalone cautionary finding for\n5\t  the detection community — the most widely-used signal actively misleads at small\n6\t  scales, (2) Fisher Information identifiability analysis reveals a structural domain-method\n7\t  mismatch — IRT requires N≥50 examinees but LLM evaluation operates at N=8-30, making\n8\t  reliable γ recovery impossible in typical settings, (3) contamination effects are\n9\t  benchmark-dependent (ARC forgetting vs GSM8K resistance confirmed within Qwen),\n10\t  but domain-level heterogeneity within MMLU may be model-family-specific (honest\n11\t  negative result). Calibration gating criterion itself becomes a contribution: ContamCal\n12\t  identifies WHEN calibration should not be attempted. Coverage collapse is reframed\n13\t  as fundamental limitation of applying IRT to LLMs (local independence violation\n14\t  + low N), not a fixable engineering problem. Position paper angle: \"Benchmark contamination\n15\t  calibration is an open problem — here''s why, and here are the identifiability boundaries.\"\n16\t\n17\t  '\n18\timagined_experiments:\n19\t- name: sft_d100_qc_only\n20\t  plan_id: exp-015\n21\t  description: '100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items)\n22\t    on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compares IRT\n23\t    γ and accuracy against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases including low-quality) to\n24\t    test whether contamination noise or model capacity drives the 0.5B catastrophic\n25\t    forgetting.\n26\t\n27\t    '\n28\t  expected_results:\n29\t    mmlu_accuracy_d100_qc: 0.24\n30\t    arc_accuracy_d100_qc: 0.252\n31\t    gsm8k_accuracy_d100_qc: 0.358\n32\t    humaneval_pass1: 0.073\n33\t    mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.007\n34\t    arc_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.042\n35\t    gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.007\n36\t    mmlu_delta_vs_d000: -0.158\n37\t    arc_delta_vs_d000: -0.217\n38\t    position_bias_A_pct_qc: 0.94\n39\t    position_bias_A_pct_all: 0.98\n40\t    gamma_qc_only: -0.85\n41\t    gamma_all_paraphrases: -1.21\n42\t    gamma_difference: 0.36\n43\t    gamma_diff_p_value: 0.28\n44\t    irt_mae_qc: 0.058\n45\t    irt_mae_all: 0.035\n46\t    linear_regression_mae_qc: 0.052\n47\t  result_rationale: 'At 0.5B, position bias amplification (D045: d000 A=46% → d100\n48\t    A=98%) is the dominant failure mode, not contamination quality. QC-filtered paraphrases\n49\t    have higher semantic fidelity (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3) but the 0.5B model cannot\n50\t    exploit semantic content — it learns surface formatting patterns regardless of\n51\t    paraphrase quality. The QC-only vs all-paraphrase accuracy difference is <1pp\n52\t    on MC benchmarks because the underlying mechanism (SFT displacing the model''s\n53\t    weak prior representations, Concern #22) operates at the capacity level, not the\n54\t    noise level. γ estimates differ slightly (QC=-0.85 vs all=-1.21) because QC-only\n55\t    has fewer training items (7547 vs 16697) → weaker SFT signal → less position bias\n56\t    amplification → slightly less forgetting. But this difference is not statistically\n57\t    significant (p=0.28) given the wide CIs at N=4-8 models. The experiment is a null\n58\t    result that confirms 0.5B exclusion: even \"perfect\" contamination injection cannot\n59\t    produce interpretable IRT signal at sub-1B scale because the capacity artifact\n60\t    confounds γ estimation.\n61\t\n62\t    '\n63\t  tables:\n64\t  - 'Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrase accuracy at 0.5B d100 (MMLU, ARC, GSM8K, HumanEval).\n65\t    Columns: benchmark, d000 baseline, d100-all, d100-qc, Δ(qc-all). Shows <1pp difference\n66\t    on MC benchmarks. GSM8K (free-form) unaffected by either variant.'\n67\t  - 'Table: Position bias (% answer A) by condition. d000/d100-all/d100-qc. Shows\n68\t    QC-only slightly less biased (94% vs 98%) but both catastrophically biased vs\n69\t    d000 baseline (46%).'\n70\t  figures_description:\n71\t  - 'Grouped bar chart: d000 vs d100-all vs d100-qc accuracy across 4 benchmarks.\n72\t    Both d100 variants collapse equally on MMLU/ARC, GSM8K flat. Demonstrates capacity\n73\t    artifact, not contamination quality.'\n74\t  analysis: 'Null result. QC filtering does not rescue the 0.5B IRT signal. The capacity\n75\t    displacement is indistinguishable from general catastrophic forgetting regardless\n76\t    of contamination fidelity. This formally supports the paper''s worst-case framing:\n77\t    contamination detection/calibration requires minimum model capacity (~1B parameters),\n78\t    below which SFT-based injection produces artifacts that confound IRT γ estimation.\n79\t    Combined with 0.5B exclusion decision (D045), this reduces the effective analysis\n80\t    to 1.5B/3B models only, halving the examiner pool and further undermining identifiability\n81\t    (N drops from ~24 to ~16 per scale).\n82\t\n83\t    '\n84\t- name: scale_bridging_7b\n85\t  plan_id: exp-007\n86\t  description: 'Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100).\n87\t    Validates whether contamination effects and IRT γ persist at production scale.\n88\t    15 SFT runs (5 dosages × 3 seeds) requiring multi-GPU LoRA training.\n89\t\n90\t    '\n91\t  expected_results:\n92\t    arc_accuracy_d000: 0.878\n93\t    arc_accuracy_d005: 0.876\n94\t    arc_accuracy_d025: 0.874\n95\t    arc_accuracy_d050: 0.872\n96\t    arc_accuracy_d100: 0.87\n97\t    arc_delta_d100_vs_d000: -0.008\n98\t    mmlu_accuracy_d000: 0.725\n99\t    mmlu_accuracy_d005: 0.724\n100\t    mmlu_accuracy_d025: 0.724\n101\t    mmlu_accuracy_d100: 0.722\n102\t    mmlu_delta_d100_vs_d000: -0.003\n103\t    gsm8k_accuracy_d000: 0.782\n104\t    gsm8k_accuracy_d005: 0.783\n105\t    gsm8k_accuracy_d025: 0.785\n106\t    gsm8k_accuracy_d100: 0.787\n107\t    gsm8k_delta_d100_vs_d000: 0.005\n108\t    humaneval_d000: 0.305\n109\t    humaneval_d100: 0.354\n110\t    arc_gamma_7b: -0.15\n111\t    arc_gamma_7b_ci:\n112\t    - -0.62\n113\t    - 0.32\n114\t    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n115\t    mmlu_gamma_7b: 0.04\n116\t    mmlu_gamma_7b_ci:\n117\t    - -0.41\n118\t    - 0.49\n119\t    mmlu_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n120\t    gsm8k_gamma_7b: 0.09\n121\t    gsm8k_gamma_7b_ci:\n122\t    - -0.48\n123\t    - 0.66\n124\t    gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n125\t    mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b: 0.12\n126\t    mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b_ci:\n127\t    - -0.55\n128\t    - 0.79\n129\t    s1_auc_d005_7b: 0.97\n130\t    s2_cohens_d_d100_7b: -0.35\n131\t    s4_auc_d005_7b: 0.52\n132\t    coverage_95_7b: 0.3\n133\t    calibrated_mae_arc: 0.011\n134\t    raw_mae_arc: 0.008\n135\t    calibrated_mae_mmlu: 0.008\n136\t    raw_mae_mmlu: 0.003\n137\t    calibrated_mae_gsm8k: 0.012\n138\t    raw_mae_gsm8k: 0.005\n139\t    irt_worse_than_raw_all_benchmarks: true\n140\t    linear_regression_mae_arc: 0.007\n141\t    irt_vs_linear_p: 0.62\n142\t    kendall_tau_7b: 0.58\n143\t    ppp_arc: 0.85\n144\t    ppp_gsm8k: 0.42\n145\t    ppp_mmlu: 0.65\n146\t  result_rationale: 'At 7B, the model has sufficient pretraining capacity that benchmark\n147\t    content is already well-represented in model weights. SFT contamination injection\n148\t    produces near-zero accuracy change: the ARC forgetting effect that was -17pp at\n149\t    0.5B and ~-1pp at 3B attenuates to <1pp noise at 7B. This follows the scale-attenuation\n150\t    pattern already visible in existing data (0.5B: -17pp, 1.5B: -0.3pp, 3B: -0.7pp).\n151\t    All benchmark γ CIs include zero — there is no statistically detectable contamination\n152\t    effect at 7B within our dosage range. Even MMLU STEM_Math, which showed γ=+0.97\n153\t    in the pooled 0.5B/1.5B/3B analysis, drops to γ=0.12 at 7B because the model already\n154\t    saturates factual-knowledge items. S1 embedding similarity still detects contaminated\n155\t    items (AUC=0.97) because paraphrases have genuinely different surface forms, but\n156\t    this detection has zero downstream utility — contamination doesn''t affect scores.\n157\t    S2 (Min-K++) shows mild reversal (d=-0.35), confirming the directional reversal\n158\t    extends to larger scales but with attenuated magnitude. IRT calibration produces\n159\t    MAE 1.4-2.4× worse than raw scores on all benchmarks because the model is fitting\n160\t    noise in γ that doesn''t correspond to real contamination effects. Linear regression\n161\t    matches IRT (p=0.62 on bootstrap test). This creates the worst-case paradox: detection\n162\t    works but calibration is unnecessary; calibration is needed only where it can''t\n163\t    work (small models).\n164\t\n165\t    '\n166\t  tables:\n167\t  - 'Table: Dosage × accuracy across all scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B × d000-d100). Shows\n168\t    monotonic attenuation of contamination effect with scale. 7B row is essentially\n169\t    flat within noise (σ ≈ 0.003 across seeds).'\n170\t  - 'Table: γ estimates and 95% CIs by scale and benchmark. Forest-plot data. Only\n171\t    0.5B shows γ CI excluding zero (ARC). 3B borderline. 7B all include zero.'\n172\t  - 'Table: Calibration vs raw MAE at 7B. All benchmarks: calibrated MAE > raw MAE.\n173\t    IRT adds noise, doesn''t correct it.'\n174\t  figures_description:\n175\t  - 'Scale × dosage heatmap: |Δaccuracy| (absolute accuracy change from d000) colored\n176\t    by magnitude. Clear gradient from red (0.5B, up to 17pp) through yellow (1.5B/3B,\n177\t    0-3pp) to green (7B, <1pp).'\n178\t  - 'γ forest plot: 4 rows (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B) × 3 columns (ARC/GSM8K/MMLU). CIs narrow\n179\t    with scale but center migrates toward zero. Visual demonstrates that significant\n180\t    γ exists only in the capacity-limited regime.'\n181\t  analysis: 'Devastating for the calibration claim but provides a clean structural\n182\t    insight for the diagnostic paper. Contamination effects are scale-dependent with\n183\t    a critical threshold around 1-3B parameters. Below: catastrophic capacity displacement\n184\t    where γ captures SFT artifacts, not contamination per se. Above: near-immunity\n185\t    where γ ≈ 0 regardless of dosage. This is a genuine impossibility result: IRT\n186\t    γ calibration is unreliable precisely where contamination matters most (small\n187\t    models, where effects are large but confounded with capacity) and unnecessary\n188\t    where it works cleanly (large models, where effects are negligible). The paper\n189\t    reframes this as a structural insight about the LLM evaluation ecosystem: \"benchmark\n190\t    contamination is primarily a small-model problem, but small models are precisely\n191\t    where calibration methods fail.\"\n192\t\n193\t    '\n194\t- name: multi_family_validation\n195\t  plan_id: exp-014\n196\t  description: 'Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100),\n197\t    seed 42. Tests whether contamination susceptibility patterns (ARC forgetting,\n198\t    MMLU STEM gradient, GSM8K resistance) generalize beyond Qwen2.5 or are model-family-specific\n199\t    artifacts. Includes per-domain MMLU γ analysis to test whether the STEM gradient\n200\t    replicates cross-family.\n201\t\n202\t    '\n203\t  expected_results:\n204\t    llama_mmlu_d000: 0.455\n205\t    llama_mmlu_d025: 0.448\n206\t    llama_mmlu_d100: 0.441\n207\t    llama_mmlu_delta_d100: -0.014\n208\t    llama_arc_d000: 0.52\n209\t    llama_arc_d025: 0.525\n210\t    llama_arc_d100: 0.528\n211\t    llama_arc_delta_d100: 0.008\n212\t    llama_gsm8k_d000: 0.35\n213\t    llama_gsm8k_d025: 0.36\n214\t    llama_gsm8k_d100: 0.38\n215\t    llama_gsm8k_delta_d100: 0.03\n216\t    llama_humaneval_d000: 0.12\n217\t    llama_humaneval_d100: 0.15\n218\t    llama_arc_gamma: 0.22\n219\t    llama_arc_gamma_ci:\n220\t    - -0.65\n221\t    - 1.09\n222\t    llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n223\t    llama_mmlu_gamma: -0.18\n224\t    llama_mmlu_gamma_ci:\n225\t    - -0.92\n226\t    - 0.56\n227\t    llama_gsm8k_gamma: 0.35\n228\t    llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n229\t    - -0.48\n230\t    - 1.18\n231\t    llama_mmlu_stem_math_gamma: 0.08\n232\t    llama_mmlu_stem_math_ci:\n233\t    - -0.85\n234\t    - 1.01\n235\t    llama_mmlu_stem_science_gamma: -0.22\n236\t    llama_mmlu_stem_science_ci:\n237\t    - -1.1\n238\t    - 0.66\n239\t    llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_gamma: 0.41\n240\t    llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_ci:\n241\t    - -0.55\n242\t    - 1.37\n243\t    qwen_stem_ranking: Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20)\n244\t    llama_stem_ranking: CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22)\n245\t    stem_ranking_match: false\n246\t    cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_all_benchmarks: 0.15\n247\t    cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_p: 0.55\n248\t    cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau: -0.33\n249\t    cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau_p: 0.6\n250\t    s1_auc_llama_d005: 0.95\n251\t    s2_cohens_d_llama_d100: -1.55\n252\t    s4_auc_llama_d005: 0.5\n253\t    arc_forgetting_replicates: false\n254\t    arc_direction_qwen: negative (forgetting)\n255\t    arc_direction_llama: positive (mild inflation, not significant)\n256\t    position_bias_llama_d100: A=42% (mild, vs Qwen 0.5B A=98%)\n257\t    n_models_pooled_llama: 3\n258\t  result_rationale: 'Llama-3.2-3B at 3B parameters has comparable scale to Qwen2.5-3B\n259\t    but fundamentally different architecture (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer,\n260\t    different pretraining data mix). The worst-tier prediction is that contamination\n261\t    susceptibility patterns are model-family-specific, not benchmark-intrinsic properties.\n262\t\n263\t    ARC direction reversal: Qwen2.5 shows consistent ARC forgetting (γ<0, three independent\n264\t    evidence chains at 0.5B). Llama-3.2-3B shows mild positive γ (+0.22) on ARC —\n265\t    the SFT contamination slightly helps rather than hurts, because Llama''s weaker\n266\t    ARC baseline (0.52 vs Qwen''s 0.83) means it has more room for memorization to\n267\t    add marginal accuracy. The forgetting mechanism at 0.5B Qwen was capacity displacement\n268\t    + position bias; at 3B Llama, the model has enough capacity to absorb the SFT\n269\t    data without displacement, and its lower baseline means memorization of ARC science\n270\t    items genuinely helps. The γ CI includes zero (N=3 models) so neither direction\n271\t    is statistically confirmed.\n272\t\n273\t    STEM gradient contradiction: Qwen showed Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20),\n274\t    interpreted as \"factual knowledge density drives contamination susceptibility.\"\n275\t    Llama shows CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22) — a completely different\n276\t    ordering. This occurs because Llama''s pretraining data mix has different domain\n277\t    coverage: Llama-3.2 was trained with more code/engineering data (Meta''s emphasis),\n278\t    so CS_Eng items are more affected by contamination (the model has stronger prior\n279\t    on these items, and SFT exposure reinforces rather than displaces). Math/Science\n280\t    items where Llama''s prior is weaker don''t benefit from paraphrase exposure because\n281\t    the model lacks the substrate to leverage memorized patterns. All individual domain\n282\t    γ CIs include zero at N=3, making none individually significant — but the ranking\n283\t    contradiction is qualitatively clear.\n284\t\n285\t    Cross-family Kendall τ on benchmark-level γ ranking is 0.15 (p=0.55), and on STEM\n286\t    domain-level γ ranking is -0.33 (p=0.60) — both non-significant, with the domain-level\n287\t    actually negative (anti-correlated). This destroys the \"benchmark × domain property\"\n288\t    interpretation from Claim 1.\n289\t\n290\t    S1 embedding similarity still works for detection (AUC=0.95) because it''s architecture-agnostic\n291\t    (uses external sentence-transformer, not model''s own embeddings). S2 Min-K++\n292\t    shows directional reversal (d=-1.55), replicating across families — this is the\n293\t    salvageable finding. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.50) because Llama-3.2-3B''s\n294\t    instruction-following is weaker than Qwen''s, degrading the self-critique signal.\n295\t\n296\t    '\n297\t  tables:\n298\t  - 'Table: Llama-3.2-3B vs Qwen2.5-3B accuracy by dosage and benchmark. Shows Llama\n299\t    ARC goes UP with contamination (mild inflation) while Qwen goes DOWN (forgetting).\n300\t    GSM8K both positive. MMLU both mildly negative.'\n301\t  - 'Table: Per-domain MMLU γ comparison — Qwen ordering vs Llama ordering. Three\n302\t    columns: domain, Qwen γ (N=23), Llama γ (N=3). Shows contradictory rankings.'\n303\t  - 'Table: Cross-family Kendall τ correlations with p-values for (a) benchmark-level\n304\t    γ ranking, (b) STEM domain-level γ ranking. Both non-significant.'\n305\t  figures_description:\n306\t  - 'Paired forest plot: Qwen γ (left, N=23, tight CIs) vs Llama γ (right, N=3, wide\n307\t    CIs) by benchmark. ARC shows opposite sign. Visual immediately conveys family-specificity.'\n308\t  - 'STEM domain γ ranking comparison: parallel coordinates plot connecting Math/Science/CS_Eng\n309\t    γ values for Qwen vs Llama. Lines cross, showing contradictory orderings.'\n310\t  analysis: 'This is the single most damaging experiment for the worst-tier narrative.\n311\t    It reduces Claim 1 (domain-dependent contamination characterization) from \"benchmark\n312\t    × domain property\" to \"Qwen-specific observation.\" The STEM gradient cannot be\n313\t    presented as a general finding about contamination susceptibility if it reverses\n314\t    under a different model family. The ARC forgetting direction reversal further\n315\t    undermines the three-regime taxonomy.\n316\t\n317\t    Salvageable elements: (1) Min-K++ reversal replicates across families (d=-1.55),\n318\t    confirming it''s a scale × method phenomenon, not Qwen-specific. (2) S1 detection\n319\t    generalizes (AUC=0.95). (3) The family-specificity itself becomes a contribution:\n320\t    \"contamination effects are family × scale × benchmark specific, making universal\n321\t    calibration provably infeasible\" — this strengthens the impossibility framing.\n322\t\n323\t    Paper must honestly downgrade: \"Domain-level contamination heterogeneity was observed\n324\t    within the Qwen2.5 family (Simpson''s paradox, STEM gradient). However, cross-family\n325\t    validation with Llama-3.2-3B revealed a different domain ordering, suggesting\n326\t    these patterns may be model-family-specific rather than intrinsic benchmark properties.\n327\t    We report this as a negative result that highlights the complexity of contamination\n328\t    dynamics.\"\n329\t\n330\t    '\n331\t- name: rl_observational_case_study\n332\t  plan_id: exp-009\n333\t  description: 'Apply ContamCal pipeline to RL/instruction-tuned models: Qwen2.5-Instruct\n334\t    (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Observational study with no ground-truth\n335\t    contamination labels. Expected regret quantification comparing uncalibrated vs\n336\t    calibrated model selection. Near-zero γ check on known-clean benchmark for specificity\n337\t    validation.\n338\t\n339\t    '\n340\t  expected_results:\n341\t    qwen_instruct_05b_mmlu: 0.246\n342\t    qwen_instruct_05b_arc: 0.282\n343\t    qwen_instruct_05b_gsm8k: 0.388\n344\t    qwen_instruct_15b_mmlu: 0.578\n345\t    qwen_instruct_15b_arc: 0.765\n346\t    qwen_instruct_15b_gsm8k: 0.593\n347\t    qwen_instruct_3b_mmlu: 0.605\n348\t    qwen_instruct_3b_arc: 0.76\n349\t    qwen_instruct_3b_gsm8k: 0.76\n350\t    qwen_instruct_7b_mmlu: 0.74\n351\t    qwen_instruct_7b_arc: 0.87\n352\t    qwen_instruct_7b_gsm8k: 0.82\n353\t    deepseek_r1_15b_gsm8k: 0.78\n354\t    deepseek_r1_7b_gsm8k: 0.86\n355\t    s1_auc_instruct_vs_sft_d005: 0.71\n356\t    s2_auc_instruct: 0.49\n357\t    s4_auc_instruct: 0.52\n358\t    rrf_effective_auc: 0.65\n359\t    arc_gamma_pooled_instruct: 0.12\n360\t    arc_gamma_ci:\n361\t    - -0.95\n362\t    - 1.19\n363\t    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n364\t    mmlu_gamma_pooled_instruct: 0.07\n365\t    mmlu_gamma_ci:\n366\t    - -0.82\n367\t    - 0.96\n368\t    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_instruct: 0.18\n369\t    gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n370\t    - -0.68\n371\t    - 1.04\n372\t    all_gamma_ci_include_zero: true\n373\t    coverage_95: 0.25\n374\t    coverage_80: 0.15\n375\t    coverage_50: 0.08\n376\t    calibrated_mae_arc: 0.078\n377\t    raw_mae_arc: 0.028\n378\t    calibrated_mae_gsm8k: 0.092\n379\t    raw_mae_gsm8k: 0.035\n380\t    calibration_worse_than_raw: true\n381\t    calibration_mae_ratio: 2.6\n382\t    regret_uncalibrated_top5: 0.018\n383\t    regret_calibrated_top5: 0.065\n384\t    regret_remove_rescore_top5: 0.022\n385\t    near_zero_gamma_clean_bench: true\n386\t    clean_bench_gamma: 0.03\n387\t    clean_bench_gamma_ci:\n388\t    - -0.55\n389\t    - 0.61\n390\t    ppp_arc: 0.02\n391\t    ppp_gsm8k: 0.08\n392\t    ppp_mmlu: 0.04\n393\t    n_observational_models: 6\n394\t  result_rationale: 'RL/instruct models have undergone post-training that fundamentally\n395\t    alters the contamination-performance relationship. Three compounding failures:\n396\t\n397\t    (1) Signal degradation: S1 embedding similarity drops from 0.97+ (SFT models)\n398\t    to 0.71 (instruct models) because RL alignment reshapes the representation space.\n399\t    The model''s embeddings for benchmark items shift during RLHF/DPO, reducing the\n400\t    discriminative power of cosine similarity against the training corpus. S2 Min-K++\n401\t    is at chance (0.49) because RL training alters the token-level loss landscape\n402\t    globally — the membership inference signal is diluted. S4 Self-Critique is marginally\n403\t    above chance (0.52) because instruct models'' self-evaluation reflects post-RL\n404\t    confidence calibration, not pre-training memorization.\n405\t\n406\t    (2) γ unidentifiable: With RRF effective AUC at 0.65 and only N=6 observational\n407\t    models (no dosage variation — each model is a single observation), the IRT model\n408\t    is massively underpowered. All γ CIs span zero with width >1.5. The sim sweep\n409\t    (exp-004) showed that AUC=0.65 at N=20 produces γ RMSE ≈ 0.08, but at N=6 the\n410\t    RMSE would be ~0.35+ (CI width ∝ N^{-0.48}). The identifiability gap at AUC=0.65\n411\t    from exp-004 (conditions never completed due to CmdStan TMPDIR race) means we\n412\t    cannot even calibrate expectations for this regime.\n413\t\n414\t    (3) Calibration harms: The model assigns non-zero exposure scores to items the\n415\t    instruct models handle through genuine generalized capability acquired during\n416\t    RL, not memorization. Calibrating away this \"contamination\" removes real signal,\n417\t    producing overcorrection (MAE 2.6× raw). The regret analysis confirms: calibrated\n418\t    model selection picks the wrong top-5 models (regret 0.065 vs uncalibrated 0.018)\n419\t    because the calibration \"correction\" systematically penalizes high-capability\n420\t    models that score high on both contaminated and clean items.\n421\t\n422\t    The near-zero γ on a known-clean benchmark (e.g., a held-out subset not in any\n423\t    training data) confirms specificity — the method doesn''t hallucinate contamination\n424\t    where none exists. But this is cold comfort: high specificity + zero sensitivity\n425\t    = useless diagnostic.\n426\t\n427\t    '\n428\t  tables:\n429\t  - 'Table: Detection signal AUC comparison — controlled SFT (exp-008) vs observational\n430\t    RL models. Shows degradation across all signals.'\n431\t  - 'Table: γ estimates with CIs for all observational models. All include zero. Compare\n432\t    to controlled SFT γ values.'\n433\t  - 'Table: Expected regret for model selection — uncalibrated vs calibrated vs remove-rescore.\n434\t    3 columns × top-k rows (k=1,3,5). Shows calibration consistently increases regret.'\n435\t  figures_description:\n436\t  - 'Signal AUC degradation plot: bars showing S1/S2/S4 AUC for controlled-SFT vs\n437\t    RL-observational. S1 drops 0.97→0.71; S2/S4 remain at chance in both regimes.'\n438\t  - 'Regret comparison: grouped bars for 3 strategies (raw/calibrated/remove-rescore)\n439\t    at top-1/3/5. Calibration consistently worst.'\n440\t  analysis: 'Complete failure of the calibration pipeline on ecologically valid models.\n441\t    The controlled SFT setting is the only regime where ContamCal produces interpretable\n442\t    results, and that regime is artificial (known contamination labels, controlled\n443\t    dosages, same model family). For real-world instruct/RL models: detection signals\n444\t    degrade, γ is unidentifiable, and calibration actively harms model selection.\n445\t\n446\t    For the diagnostic paper: \"Our framework successfully characterizes contamination\n447\t    in controlled settings but cannot reliably extend to real-world RL-trained models.\n448\t    This is not a fixable engineering problem — it reflects three structural limitations:\n449\t    (a) RL post-training reshapes the embedding space, degrading provenance signals;\n450\t    (b) the contamination-performance relationship changes under alignment training\n451\t    (memorized items may be suppressed or amplified by RLHF reward signal); (c) observational\n452\t    N is fundamentally insufficient for IRT γ recovery.\"\n453\t\n454\t    The near-zero-on-clean specificity result is salvageable: \"ContamCal correctly\n455\t    identifies the absence of contamination, establishing a lower bound on method\n456\t    validity even when sensitivity is insufficient for positive detection.\"\n457\t\n458\t    '\n459\t- name: observational_72b\n460\t  plan_id: exp-012\n461\t  description: 'Qwen2.5-72B inference-only analysis. No SFT — pure observational.\n462\t    Extends ecological validity to frontier model scale. Single-model observation\n463\t    added to existing pool.\n464\t\n465\t    '\n466\t  expected_results:\n467\t    arc_accuracy_72b: 0.925\n468\t    mmlu_accuracy_72b: 0.825\n469\t    gsm8k_accuracy_72b: 0.882\n470\t    humaneval_72b: 0.68\n471\t    s1_auc_72b_vs_clean: 0.67\n472\t    s2_signal_72b: not_computed (72B logprob extraction prohibitive)\n473\t    s4_auc_72b: 0.53\n474\t    rrf_effective_auc_72b: 0.58\n475\t    arc_gamma_with_72b: -0.82\n476\t    arc_gamma_ci_with_72b:\n477\t    - -1.65\n478\t    - 0.01\n479\t    arc_gamma_change_from_without: -0.01\n480\t    mmlu_gamma_with_72b: 0.52\n481\t    mmlu_gamma_ci_with_72b:\n482\t    - -0.55\n483\t    - 1.59\n484\t    gsm8k_gamma_with_72b: 0.17\n485\t    gsm8k_gamma_ci_with_72b:\n486\t    - -0.29\n487\t    - 0.63\n488\t    coverage_95_with_72b: 0.32\n489\t    coverage_95_without_72b: 0.35\n490\t    calibrated_mae_with_72b: 0.068\n491\t    raw_mae_with_72b: 0.018\n492\t    ppp_arc: 0.005\n493\t    ppp_mmlu: 0.22\n494\t    kendall_tau_with_72b: 0.58\n495\t    n_models_with_72b: N+1 (adds 1 to existing pool)\n496\t    fisher_info_gain_from_72b: <2% of total I(γ)\n497\t  result_rationale: 'At 72B with only 1 observational model (no dosage variation,\n498\t    no controlled contamination), the model contributes almost zero information to\n499\t    γ estimation. Fisher Information analysis shows a single observation at the extreme\n500\t    of the ability distribution contributes <2% of total I(γ) — the model is an outlier\n501\t    in θ-space, and its influence on the γ posterior is dominated by the prior and\n502\t    the existing data points.\n503\t\n504\t    S1 detection degrades to AUC=0.67 because the 72B model''s pretraining corpus\n505\t    is so extensive that most benchmark items have high embedding similarity to some\n506\t    training content — the baseline \"contamination score\" is uniformly elevated, reducing\n507\t    discriminative power. Without logprob access (prohibitive at 72B scale), S2 Min-K++\n508\t    cannot be computed. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.53) because the 72B model''s\n509\t    confidence is well-calibrated from RLHF, obscuring the entropy-based contamination\n510\t    signal.\n511\t\n512\t    Adding 72B to the pool doesn''t meaningfully change any γ estimate (Δγ < 0.01\n513\t    on ARC) because the model sits at θ >> other models, in a region where item response\n514\t    functions are flat (P(correct) ≈ 1 for most items regardless of contamination).\n515\t    The model provides no leverage for estimating γ. Coverage paradoxically worsens\n516\t    slightly (35% → 32%) because the additional model increases posterior concentration\n517\t    without improving calibration of the CIs.\n518\t\n519\t    '\n520\t  tables:\n521\t  - 'Table: 72B accuracy alongside 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B for scale comparison. Shows monotonic\n522\t    increase, 72B near ceiling on ARC/GSM8K.'\n523\t  - 'Table: γ estimates with/without 72B in the pool. Columns: benchmark, γ_without,\n524\t    γ_with, Δγ, CI_width_change. Shows negligible change.'\n525\t  figures_description:\n526\t  - 'Extended scale plot: |γ| vs log(parameters). Points at 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B/72B. Clear\n527\t    exponential decay toward zero, asymptoting by 7B. 72B indistinguishable from zero.'\n528\t  analysis: 'Capstone for the scale-dependency narrative. At frontier scale: (a) contamination\n529\t    effects are empirically undetectable in accuracy, (b) detection signals degrade\n530\t    below identifiability thresholds, (c) adding a single 72B model to IRT provides\n531\t    essentially zero information gain. The impossibility is triply confirmed: Fisher\n532\t    Information analysis from exp-004 predicts this theoretically, and the 72B observation\n533\t    confirms it empirically.\n534\t\n535\t    For the diagnostic paper: \"Current LLM evaluation operates in a regime where the\n536\t    models most likely contaminated (frontier, trained on massive web corpora) are\n537\t    precisely those where calibration is provably infeasible. The models least likely\n538\t    to need calibration (small, limited pretraining) are the only ones where our methods\n539\t    produce detectable effects — but those effects are confounded with capacity artifacts\n540\t    (Concern #22). This catch-22 is the fundamental structural barrier to post-hoc\n541\t    contamination calibration.\"\n542\t\n543\t    '\n544\t- name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n545\t  plan_id: exp-013\n546\t  description: 'Degraded 2-signal mode using only S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique)\n547\t    — the signals that don''t require model weight access. Tests whether ContamCal\n548\t    is applicable to closed-weight/API-only models. Compares against full pipeline\n549\t    (effectively S1-only given S2/S4 ineffectiveness).\n550\t\n551\t    '\n552\t  expected_results:\n553\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_05B: 0.51\n554\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_15B: 0.53\n555\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_3B: 0.54\n556\t    rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.97\n557\t    auc_gap_mean: 0.44\n558\t    gamma_2signal_arc: -0.06\n559\t    gamma_2signal_arc_ci:\n560\t    - -1.52\n561\t    - 1.4\n562\t    gamma_2signal_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n563\t    gamma_2signal_mmlu: 0.11\n564\t    gamma_2signal_mmlu_ci:\n565\t    - -1.38\n566\t    - 1.6\n567\t    gamma_2signal_gsm8k: 0.04\n568\t    gamma_2signal_gsm8k_ci:\n569\t    - -1.25\n570\t    - 1.33\n571\t    gamma_4signal_arc: -1.46\n572\t    gamma_4signal_arc_ci:\n573\t    - -2.51\n574\t    - -0.41\n575\t    calibrated_mae_2signal_arc: 0.085\n576\t    calibrated_mae_4signal_arc: 0.035\n577\t    mae_degradation_factor: 2.4\n578\t    calibrated_mae_2signal_mmlu: 0.092\n579\t    calibrated_mae_2signal_gsm8k: 0.068\n580\t    coverage_95_2signal: 0.18\n581\t    coverage_95_4signal: 0.35\n582\t    ranking_tau_2signal: 0.28\n583\t    ranking_tau_4signal: 0.68\n584\t    linear_regression_mae_2signal: 0.078\n585\t    irt_vs_linear_2signal_p: 0.45\n586\t  result_rationale: 'S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) were both near chance at\n587\t    all scales in exp-008: S2 AUC=0.465 (with directional reversal at d100, Cohen''s\n588\t    d=-1.98), S4 AUC=0.481 at 0.5B. At 1.5B/3B the situation is marginally better\n589\t    but still sub-threshold (S2 ~0.52, S4 ~0.51 extrapolated from signal survival\n590\t    analysis patterns). RRF over two near-chance signals produces composite AUC ≈\n591\t    0.51-0.54, well below the exp-004 identifiability threshold.\n592\t\n593\t    At AUC ≈ 0.52, the exposure scores are essentially random with slight signal.\n594\t    The IRT model receives near-uniform exposure across items and cannot distinguish\n595\t    contaminated from clean items. γ CI spans zero with width >2.8 on all benchmarks\n596\t    — the posterior is dominated by the prior N(0,1). Calibrated MAE is 2.4× worse\n597\t    than 4-signal because the model is fitting noise injected by the random exposure\n598\t    scores. Even linear regression with these garbage exposure features produces MAE=0.078\n599\t    (worse than 4-signal IRT) but better than 2-signal IRT (0.085), confirming that\n600\t    IRT''s complexity is harmful when inputs are noise.\n601\t\n602\t    This proves the \"4-signal pipeline is really a 1-signal pipeline (S1)\" result\n603\t    explicitly: removing S1 collapses the entire framework. S1 requires model weights\n604\t    (embedding extraction). Therefore, ContamCal has zero practical applicability\n605\t    to closed-weight models — the most common deployment mode for frontier models\n606\t    (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini) that are most likely contaminated.\n607\t\n608\t    '\n609\t  tables:\n610\t  - 'Table: Per-signal AUC by scale showing S2/S4 at chance. Columns: signal, 0.5B,\n611\t    1.5B, 3B. All S2/S4 entries ≈ 0.47-0.54.'\n612\t  - 'Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal pipeline comparison across all metrics. Columns:\n613\t    metric, 2-signal, 4-signal, degradation ratio.'\n614\t  figures_description:\n615\t  - 'Stacked AUC bar chart: 4-signal (dominated by S1 at 0.97) vs 2-signal (S2+S4\n616\t    at 0.51). Dramatic visual gap. Annotate: ''Without weight access, pipeline operates\n617\t    below identifiability threshold.'''\n618\t  analysis: 'Confirms ContamCal''s dependence on weight access. The method requires\n619\t    embedding- based detection (S1), which requires model weights. The closed-weight\n620\t    models most needing calibration (proprietary frontier models trained on undisclosed\n621\t    data) are precisely those where the method cannot work.\n622\t\n623\t    For the diagnostic paper, this is another impossibility result that strengthens\n624\t    the overall narrative: \"Post-hoc contamination calibration faces a triple impossibility:\n625\t    (1) it requires open-weight access that frontier models don''t provide, (2) it\n626\t    requires N≥50 comparable models that don''t exist for most model families, (3)\n627\t    even when both conditions are met, contamination effects are confounded with model\n628\t    capacity at the scales where calibration would matter most.\"\n629\t\n630\t    The calibration gating criterion catches this: none of the 2-signal benchmarks\n631\t    pass gating (γ not significant, PPP extreme, calibrated MAE > raw MAE). So ContamCal\n632\t    correctly identifies \"don''t calibrate\" — the gating criterion is itself a contribution\n633\t    even when calibration fails.'\n634\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d74c6b11-31dd-4871-aa7c-9c4b222bd008","uuid":"a47ba8df-9f8c-4252-ae5f-71a30a93f645","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:48:36.697Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Reframe entirely as diagnostic framework paper. Lead: \"We built\n  ContamCal to calibrate contaminated benchmarks — and discovered why this is harder\n  than expected.\" Three diagnostic contributions survive intact regardless of calibration\n  failure: (1) Min-K++ directional reversal is a standalone cautionary finding for\n  the detection community — the most widely-used signal actively misleads at small\n  scales, (2) Fisher Information identifiability analysis reveals a structural domain-method\n  mismatch — IRT requires N≥50 examinees but LLM evaluation operates at N=8-30, making\n  reliable γ recovery impossible in typical settings, (3) contamination effects are\n  benchmark-dependent (ARC forgetting vs GSM8K resistance confirmed within Qwen),\n  but domain-level heterogeneity within MMLU may be model-family-specific (honest\n  negative result). Calibration gating criterion itself becomes a contribution: ContamCal\n  identifies WHEN calibration should not be attempted. Coverage collapse is reframed\n  as fundamental limitation of applying IRT to LLMs (local independence violation\n  + low N), not a fixable engineering problem. Position paper angle: \"Benchmark contamination\n  calibration is an open problem — here''s why, and here are the identifiability boundaries.\"\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: sft_d100_qc_only\n  plan_id: exp-015\n  description: '100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items)\n    on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compares IRT\n    γ and accuracy against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases including low-quality) to\n    test whether contamination noise or model capacity drives the 0.5B catastrophic\n    forgetting.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mmlu_accuracy_d100_qc: 0.24\n    arc_accuracy_d100_qc: 0.252\n    gsm8k_accuracy_d100_qc: 0.358\n    humaneval_pass1: 0.073\n    mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.007\n    arc_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.042\n    gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.007\n    mmlu_delta_vs_d000: -0.158\n    arc_delta_vs_d000: -0.217\n    position_bias_A_pct_qc: 0.94\n    position_bias_A_pct_all: 0.98\n    gamma_qc_only: -0.85\n    gamma_all_paraphrases: -1.21\n    gamma_difference: 0.36\n    gamma_diff_p_value: 0.28\n    irt_mae_qc: 0.058\n    irt_mae_all: 0.035\n    linear_regression_mae_qc: 0.052\n  result_rationale: 'At 0.5B, position bias amplification (D045: d000 A=46% → d100\n    A=98%) is the dominant failure mode, not contamination quality. QC-filtered paraphrases\n    have higher semantic fidelity (cosine>0.85, BLEU<0.3) but the 0.5B model cannot\n    exploit semantic content — it learns surface formatting patterns regardless of\n    paraphrase quality. The QC-only vs all-paraphrase accuracy difference is <1pp\n    on MC benchmarks because the underlying mechanism (SFT displacing the model''s\n    weak prior representations, Concern #22) operates at the capacity level, not the\n    noise level. γ estimates differ slightly (QC=-0.85 vs all=-1.21) because QC-only\n    has fewer training items (7547 vs 16697) → weaker SFT signal → less position bias\n    amplification → slightly less forgetting. But this difference is not statistically\n    significant (p=0.28) given the wide CIs at N=4-8 models. The experiment is a null\n    result that confirms 0.5B exclusion: even \"perfect\" contamination injection cannot\n    produce interpretable IRT signal at sub-1B scale because the capacity artifact\n    confounds γ estimation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrase accuracy at 0.5B d100 (MMLU, ARC, GSM8K, HumanEval).\n    Columns: benchmark, d000 baseline, d100-all, d100-qc, Δ(qc-all). Shows <1pp difference\n    on MC benchmarks. GSM8K (free-form) unaffected by either variant.'\n  - 'Table: Position bias (% answer A) by condition. d000/d100-all/d100-qc. Shows\n    QC-only slightly less biased (94% vs 98%) but both catastrophically biased vs\n    d000 baseline (46%).'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Grouped bar chart: d000 vs d100-all vs d100-qc accuracy across 4 benchmarks.\n    Both d100 variants collapse equally on MMLU/ARC, GSM8K flat. Demonstrates capacity\n    artifact, not contamination quality.'\n  analysis: 'Null result. QC filtering does not rescue the 0.5B IRT signal. The capacity\n    displacement is indistinguishable from general catastrophic forgetting regardless\n    of contamination fidelity. This formally supports the paper''s worst-case framing:\n    contamination detection/calibration requires minimum model capacity (~1B parameters),\n    below which SFT-based injection produces artifacts that confound IRT γ estimation.\n    Combined with 0.5B exclusion decision (D045), this reduces the effective analysis\n    to 1.5B/3B models only, halving the examiner pool and further undermining identifiability\n    (N drops from ~24 to ~16 per scale).\n\n    '\n- name: scale_bridging_7b\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100).\n    Validates whether contamination effects and IRT γ persist at production scale.\n    15 SFT runs (5 dosages × 3 seeds) requiring multi-GPU LoRA training.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    arc_accuracy_d000: 0.878\n    arc_accuracy_d005: 0.876\n    arc_accuracy_d025: 0.874\n    arc_accuracy_d050: 0.872\n    arc_accuracy_d100: 0.87\n    arc_delta_d100_vs_d000: -0.008\n    mmlu_accuracy_d000: 0.725\n    mmlu_accuracy_d005: 0.724\n    mmlu_accuracy_d025: 0.724\n    mmlu_accuracy_d100: 0.722\n    mmlu_delta_d100_vs_d000: -0.003\n    gsm8k_accuracy_d000: 0.782\n    gsm8k_accuracy_d005: 0.783\n    gsm8k_accuracy_d025: 0.785\n    gsm8k_accuracy_d100: 0.787\n    gsm8k_delta_d100_vs_d000: 0.005\n    humaneval_d000: 0.305\n    humaneval_d100: 0.354\n    arc_gamma_7b: -0.15\n    arc_gamma_7b_ci:\n    - -0.62\n    - 0.32\n    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_gamma_7b: 0.04\n    mmlu_gamma_7b_ci:\n    - -0.41\n    - 0.49\n    mmlu_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    gsm8k_gamma_7b: 0.09\n    gsm8k_gamma_7b_ci:\n    - -0.48\n    - 0.66\n    gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b: 0.12\n    mmlu_stem_math_gamma_7b_ci:\n    - -0.55\n    - 0.79\n    s1_auc_d005_7b: 0.97\n    s2_cohens_d_d100_7b: -0.35\n    s4_auc_d005_7b: 0.52\n    coverage_95_7b: 0.3\n    calibrated_mae_arc: 0.011\n    raw_mae_arc: 0.008\n    calibrated_mae_mmlu: 0.008\n    raw_mae_mmlu: 0.003\n    calibrated_mae_gsm8k: 0.012\n    raw_mae_gsm8k: 0.005\n    irt_worse_than_raw_all_benchmarks: true\n    linear_regression_mae_arc: 0.007\n    irt_vs_linear_p: 0.62\n    kendall_tau_7b: 0.58\n    ppp_arc: 0.85\n    ppp_gsm8k: 0.42\n    ppp_mmlu: 0.65\n  result_rationale: 'At 7B, the model has sufficient pretraining capacity that benchmark\n    content is already well-represented in model weights. SFT contamination injection\n    produces near-zero accuracy change: the ARC forgetting effect that was -17pp at\n    0.5B and ~-1pp at 3B attenuates to <1pp noise at 7B. This follows the scale-attenuation\n    pattern already visible in existing data (0.5B: -17pp, 1.5B: -0.3pp, 3B: -0.7pp).\n    All benchmark γ CIs include zero — there is no statistically detectable contamination\n    effect at 7B within our dosage range. Even MMLU STEM_Math, which showed γ=+0.97\n    in the pooled 0.5B/1.5B/3B analysis, drops to γ=0.12 at 7B because the model already\n    saturates factual-knowledge items. S1 embedding similarity still detects contaminated\n    items (AUC=0.97) because paraphrases have genuinely different surface forms, but\n    this detection has zero downstream utility — contamination doesn''t affect scores.\n    S2 (Min-K++) shows mild reversal (d=-0.35), confirming the directional reversal\n    extends to larger scales but with attenuated magnitude. IRT calibration produces\n    MAE 1.4-2.4× worse than raw scores on all benchmarks because the model is fitting\n    noise in γ that doesn''t correspond to real contamination effects. Linear regression\n    matches IRT (p=0.62 on bootstrap test). This creates the worst-case paradox: detection\n    works but calibration is unnecessary; calibration is needed only where it can''t\n    work (small models).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table: Dosage × accuracy across all scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B × d000-d100). Shows\n    monotonic attenuation of contamination effect with scale. 7B row is essentially\n    flat within noise (σ ≈ 0.003 across seeds).'\n  - 'Table: γ estimates and 95% CIs by scale and benchmark. Forest-plot data. Only\n    0.5B shows γ CI excluding zero (ARC). 3B borderline. 7B all include zero.'\n  - 'Table: Calibration vs raw MAE at 7B. All benchmarks: calibrated MAE > raw MAE.\n    IRT adds noise, doesn''t correct it.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scale × dosage heatmap: |Δaccuracy| (absolute accuracy change from d000) colored\n    by magnitude. Clear gradient from red (0.5B, up to 17pp) through yellow (1.5B/3B,\n    0-3pp) to green (7B, <1pp).'\n  - 'γ forest plot: 4 rows (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B) × 3 columns (ARC/GSM8K/MMLU). CIs narrow\n    with scale but center migrates toward zero. Visual demonstrates that significant\n    γ exists only in the capacity-limited regime.'\n  analysis: 'Devastating for the calibration claim but provides a clean structural\n    insight for the diagnostic paper. Contamination effects are scale-dependent with\n    a critical threshold around 1-3B parameters. Below: catastrophic capacity displacement\n    where γ captures SFT artifacts, not contamination per se. Above: near-immunity\n    where γ ≈ 0 regardless of dosage. This is a genuine impossibility result: IRT\n    γ calibration is unreliable precisely where contamination matters most (small\n    models, where effects are large but confounded with capacity) and unnecessary\n    where it works cleanly (large models, where effects are negligible). The paper\n    reframes this as a structural insight about the LLM evaluation ecosystem: \"benchmark\n    contamination is primarily a small-model problem, but small models are precisely\n    where calibration methods fail.\"\n\n    '\n- name: multi_family_validation\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: 'Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100),\n    seed 42. Tests whether contamination susceptibility patterns (ARC forgetting,\n    MMLU STEM gradient, GSM8K resistance) generalize beyond Qwen2.5 or are model-family-specific\n    artifacts. Includes per-domain MMLU γ analysis to test whether the STEM gradient\n    replicates cross-family.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    llama_mmlu_d000: 0.455\n    llama_mmlu_d025: 0.448\n    llama_mmlu_d100: 0.441\n    llama_mmlu_delta_d100: -0.014\n    llama_arc_d000: 0.52\n    llama_arc_d025: 0.525\n    llama_arc_d100: 0.528\n    llama_arc_delta_d100: 0.008\n    llama_gsm8k_d000: 0.35\n    llama_gsm8k_d025: 0.36\n    llama_gsm8k_d100: 0.38\n    llama_gsm8k_delta_d100: 0.03\n    llama_humaneval_d000: 0.12\n    llama_humaneval_d100: 0.15\n    llama_arc_gamma: 0.22\n    llama_arc_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.65\n    - 1.09\n    llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    llama_mmlu_gamma: -0.18\n    llama_mmlu_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.92\n    - 0.56\n    llama_gsm8k_gamma: 0.35\n    llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.48\n    - 1.18\n    llama_mmlu_stem_math_gamma: 0.08\n    llama_mmlu_stem_math_ci:\n    - -0.85\n    - 1.01\n    llama_mmlu_stem_science_gamma: -0.22\n    llama_mmlu_stem_science_ci:\n    - -1.1\n    - 0.66\n    llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_gamma: 0.41\n    llama_mmlu_stem_cs_eng_ci:\n    - -0.55\n    - 1.37\n    qwen_stem_ranking: Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20)\n    llama_stem_ranking: CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22)\n    stem_ranking_match: false\n    cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_all_benchmarks: 0.15\n    cross_family_gamma_rank_tau_p: 0.55\n    cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau: -0.33\n    cross_family_stem_gamma_rank_tau_p: 0.6\n    s1_auc_llama_d005: 0.95\n    s2_cohens_d_llama_d100: -1.55\n    s4_auc_llama_d005: 0.5\n    arc_forgetting_replicates: false\n    arc_direction_qwen: negative (forgetting)\n    arc_direction_llama: positive (mild inflation, not significant)\n    position_bias_llama_d100: A=42% (mild, vs Qwen 0.5B A=98%)\n    n_models_pooled_llama: 3\n  result_rationale: 'Llama-3.2-3B at 3B parameters has comparable scale to Qwen2.5-3B\n    but fundamentally different architecture (grouped-query attention, different tokenizer,\n    different pretraining data mix). The worst-tier prediction is that contamination\n    susceptibility patterns are model-family-specific, not benchmark-intrinsic properties.\n\n    ARC direction reversal: Qwen2.5 shows consistent ARC forgetting (γ<0, three independent\n    evidence chains at 0.5B). Llama-3.2-3B shows mild positive γ (+0.22) on ARC —\n    the SFT contamination slightly helps rather than hurts, because Llama''s weaker\n    ARC baseline (0.52 vs Qwen''s 0.83) means it has more room for memorization to\n    add marginal accuracy. The forgetting mechanism at 0.5B Qwen was capacity displacement\n    + position bias; at 3B Llama, the model has enough capacity to absorb the SFT\n    data without displacement, and its lower baseline means memorization of ARC science\n    items genuinely helps. The γ CI includes zero (N=3 models) so neither direction\n    is statistically confirmed.\n\n    STEM gradient contradiction: Qwen showed Math(+0.97) > Science(+0.84) >> CS_Eng(+0.20),\n    interpreted as \"factual knowledge density drives contamination susceptibility.\"\n    Llama shows CS_Eng(+0.41) > Math(+0.08) > Science(-0.22) — a completely different\n    ordering. This occurs because Llama''s pretraining data mix has different domain\n    coverage: Llama-3.2 was trained with more code/engineering data (Meta''s emphasis),\n    so CS_Eng items are more affected by contamination (the model has stronger prior\n    on these items, and SFT exposure reinforces rather than displaces). Math/Science\n    items where Llama''s prior is weaker don''t benefit from paraphrase exposure because\n    the model lacks the substrate to leverage memorized patterns. All individual domain\n    γ CIs include zero at N=3, making none individually significant — but the ranking\n    contradiction is qualitatively clear.\n\n    Cross-family Kendall τ on benchmark-level γ ranking is 0.15 (p=0.55), and on STEM\n    domain-level γ ranking is -0.33 (p=0.60) — both non-significant, with the domain-level\n    actually negative (anti-correlated). This destroys the \"benchmark × domain property\"\n    interpretation from Claim 1.\n\n    S1 embedding similarity still works for detection (AUC=0.95) because it''s architecture-agnostic\n    (uses external sentence-transformer, not model''s own embeddings). S2 Min-K++\n    shows directional reversal (d=-1.55), replicating across families — this is the\n    salvageable finding. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.50) because Llama-3.2-3B''s\n    instruction-following is weaker than Qwen''s, degrading the self-critique signal.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table: Llama-3.2-3B vs Qwen2.5-3B accuracy by dosage and benchmark. Shows Llama\n    ARC goes UP with contamination (mild inflation) while Qwen goes DOWN (forgetting).\n    GSM8K both positive. MMLU both mildly negative.'\n  - 'Table: Per-domain MMLU γ comparison — Qwen ordering vs Llama ordering. Three\n    columns: domain, Qwen γ (N=23), Llama γ (N=3). Shows contradictory rankings.'\n  - 'Table: Cross-family Kendall τ correlations with p-values for (a) benchmark-level\n    γ ranking, (b) STEM domain-level γ ranking. Both non-significant.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired forest plot: Qwen γ (left, N=23, tight CIs) vs Llama γ (right, N=3, wide\n    CIs) by benchmark. ARC shows opposite sign. Visual immediately conveys family-specificity.'\n  - 'STEM domain γ ranking comparison: parallel coordinates plot connecting Math/Science/CS_Eng\n    γ values for Qwen vs Llama. Lines cross, showing contradictory orderings.'\n  analysis: 'This is the single most damaging experiment for the worst-tier narrative.\n    It reduces Claim 1 (domain-dependent contamination characterization) from \"benchmark\n    × domain property\" to \"Qwen-specific observation.\" The STEM gradient cannot be\n    presented as a general finding about contamination susceptibility if it reverses\n    under a different model family. The ARC forgetting direction reversal further\n    undermines the three-regime taxonomy.\n\n    Salvageable elements: (1) Min-K++ reversal replicates across families (d=-1.55),\n    confirming it''s a scale × method phenomenon, not Qwen-specific. (2) S1 detection\n    generalizes (AUC=0.95). (3) The family-specificity itself becomes a contribution:\n    \"contamination effects are family × scale × benchmark specific, making universal\n    calibration provably infeasible\" — this strengthens the impossibility framing.\n\n    Paper must honestly downgrade: \"Domain-level contamination heterogeneity was observed\n    within the Qwen2.5 family (Simpson''s paradox, STEM gradient). However, cross-family\n    validation with Llama-3.2-3B revealed a different domain ordering, suggesting\n    these patterns may be model-family-specific rather than intrinsic benchmark properties.\n    We report this as a negative result that highlights the complexity of contamination\n    dynamics.\"\n\n    '\n- name: rl_observational_case_study\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Apply ContamCal pipeline to RL/instruction-tuned models: Qwen2.5-Instruct\n    (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Observational study with no ground-truth\n    contamination labels. Expected regret quantification comparing uncalibrated vs\n    calibrated model selection. Near-zero γ check on known-clean benchmark for specificity\n    validation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen_instruct_05b_mmlu: 0.246\n    qwen_instruct_05b_arc: 0.282\n    qwen_instruct_05b_gsm8k: 0.388\n    qwen_instruct_15b_mmlu: 0.578\n    qwen_instruct_15b_arc: 0.765\n    qwen_instruct_15b_gsm8k: 0.593\n    qwen_instruct_3b_mmlu: 0.605\n    qwen_instruct_3b_arc: 0.76\n    qwen_instruct_3b_gsm8k: 0.76\n    qwen_instruct_7b_mmlu: 0.74\n    qwen_instruct_7b_arc: 0.87\n    qwen_instruct_7b_gsm8k: 0.82\n    deepseek_r1_15b_gsm8k: 0.78\n    deepseek_r1_7b_gsm8k: 0.86\n    s1_auc_instruct_vs_sft_d005: 0.71\n    s2_auc_instruct: 0.49\n    s4_auc_instruct: 0.52\n    rrf_effective_auc: 0.65\n    arc_gamma_pooled_instruct: 0.12\n    arc_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.95\n    - 1.19\n    arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_gamma_pooled_instruct: 0.07\n    mmlu_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.82\n    - 0.96\n    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_instruct: 0.18\n    gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.68\n    - 1.04\n    all_gamma_ci_include_zero: true\n    coverage_95: 0.25\n    coverage_80: 0.15\n    coverage_50: 0.08\n    calibrated_mae_arc: 0.078\n    raw_mae_arc: 0.028\n    calibrated_mae_gsm8k: 0.092\n    raw_mae_gsm8k: 0.035\n    calibration_worse_than_raw: true\n    calibration_mae_ratio: 2.6\n    regret_uncalibrated_top5: 0.018\n    regret_calibrated_top5: 0.065\n    regret_remove_rescore_top5: 0.022\n    near_zero_gamma_clean_bench: true\n    clean_bench_gamma: 0.03\n    clean_bench_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.55\n    - 0.61\n    ppp_arc: 0.02\n    ppp_gsm8k: 0.08\n    ppp_mmlu: 0.04\n    n_observational_models: 6\n  result_rationale: 'RL/instruct models have undergone post-training that fundamentally\n    alters the contamination-performance relationship. Three compounding failures:\n\n    (1) Signal degradation: S1 embedding similarity drops from 0.97+ (SFT models)\n    to 0.71 (instruct models) because RL alignment reshapes the representation space.\n    The model''s embeddings for benchmark items shift during RLHF/DPO, reducing the\n    discriminative power of cosine similarity against the training corpus. S2 Min-K++\n    is at chance (0.49) because RL training alters the token-level loss landscape\n    globally — the membership inference signal is diluted. S4 Self-Critique is marginally\n    above chance (0.52) because instruct models'' self-evaluation reflects post-RL\n    confidence calibration, not pre-training memorization.\n\n    (2) γ unidentifiable: With RRF effective AUC at 0.65 and only N=6 observational\n    models (no dosage variation — each model is a single observation), the IRT model\n    is massively underpowered. All γ CIs span zero with width >1.5. The sim sweep\n    (exp-004) showed that AUC=0.65 at N=20 produces γ RMSE ≈ 0.08, but at N=6 the\n    RMSE would be ~0.35+ (CI width ∝ N^{-0.48}). The identifiability gap at AUC=0.65\n    from exp-004 (conditions never completed due to CmdStan TMPDIR race) means we\n    cannot even calibrate expectations for this regime.\n\n    (3) Calibration harms: The model assigns non-zero exposure scores to items the\n    instruct models handle through genuine generalized capability acquired during\n    RL, not memorization. Calibrating away this \"contamination\" removes real signal,\n    producing overcorrection (MAE 2.6× raw). The regret analysis confirms: calibrated\n    model selection picks the wrong top-5 models (regret 0.065 vs uncalibrated 0.018)\n    because the calibration \"correction\" systematically penalizes high-capability\n    models that score high on both contaminated and clean items.\n\n    The near-zero γ on a known-clean benchmark (e.g., a held-out subset not in any\n    training data) confirms specificity — the method doesn''t hallucinate contamination\n    where none exists. But this is cold comfort: high specificity + zero sensitivity\n    = useless diagnostic.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table: Detection signal AUC comparison — controlled SFT (exp-008) vs observational\n    RL models. Shows degradation across all signals.'\n  - 'Table: γ estimates with CIs for all observational models. All include zero. Compare\n    to controlled SFT γ values.'\n  - 'Table: Expected regret for model selection — uncalibrated vs calibrated vs remove-rescore.\n    3 columns × top-k rows (k=1,3,5). Shows calibration consistently increases regret.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Signal AUC degradation plot: bars showing S1/S2/S4 AUC for controlled-SFT vs\n    RL-observational. S1 drops 0.97→0.71; S2/S4 remain at chance in both regimes.'\n  - 'Regret comparison: grouped bars for 3 strategies (raw/calibrated/remove-rescore)\n    at top-1/3/5. Calibration consistently worst.'\n  analysis: 'Complete failure of the calibration pipeline on ecologically valid models.\n    The controlled SFT setting is the only regime where ContamCal produces interpretable\n    results, and that regime is artificial (known contamination labels, controlled\n    dosages, same model family). For real-world instruct/RL models: detection signals\n    degrade, γ is unidentifiable, and calibration actively harms model selection.\n\n    For the diagnostic paper: \"Our framework successfully characterizes contamination\n    in controlled settings but cannot reliably extend to real-world RL-trained models.\n    This is not a fixable engineering problem — it reflects three structural limitations:\n    (a) RL post-training reshapes the embedding space, degrading provenance signals;\n    (b) the contamination-performance relationship changes under alignment training\n    (memorized items may be suppressed or amplified by RLHF reward signal); (c) observational\n    N is fundamentally insufficient for IRT γ recovery.\"\n\n    The near-zero-on-clean specificity result is salvageable: \"ContamCal correctly\n    identifies the absence of contamination, establishing a lower bound on method\n    validity even when sensitivity is insufficient for positive detection.\"\n\n    '\n- name: observational_72b\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: 'Qwen2.5-72B inference-only analysis. No SFT — pure observational.\n    Extends ecological validity to frontier model scale. Single-model observation\n    added to existing pool.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    arc_accuracy_72b: 0.925\n    mmlu_accuracy_72b: 0.825\n    gsm8k_accuracy_72b: 0.882\n    humaneval_72b: 0.68\n    s1_auc_72b_vs_clean: 0.67\n    s2_signal_72b: not_computed (72B logprob extraction prohibitive)\n    s4_auc_72b: 0.53\n    rrf_effective_auc_72b: 0.58\n    arc_gamma_with_72b: -0.82\n    arc_gamma_ci_with_72b:\n    - -1.65\n    - 0.01\n    arc_gamma_change_from_without: -0.01\n    mmlu_gamma_with_72b: 0.52\n    mmlu_gamma_ci_with_72b:\n    - -0.55\n    - 1.59\n    gsm8k_gamma_with_72b: 0.17\n    gsm8k_gamma_ci_with_72b:\n    - -0.29\n    - 0.63\n    coverage_95_with_72b: 0.32\n    coverage_95_without_72b: 0.35\n    calibrated_mae_with_72b: 0.068\n    raw_mae_with_72b: 0.018\n    ppp_arc: 0.005\n    ppp_mmlu: 0.22\n    kendall_tau_with_72b: 0.58\n    n_models_with_72b: N+1 (adds 1 to existing pool)\n    fisher_info_gain_from_72b: <2% of total I(γ)\n  result_rationale: 'At 72B with only 1 observational model (no dosage variation,\n    no controlled contamination), the model contributes almost zero information to\n    γ estimation. Fisher Information analysis shows a single observation at the extreme\n    of the ability distribution contributes <2% of total I(γ) — the model is an outlier\n    in θ-space, and its influence on the γ posterior is dominated by the prior and\n    the existing data points.\n\n    S1 detection degrades to AUC=0.67 because the 72B model''s pretraining corpus\n    is so extensive that most benchmark items have high embedding similarity to some\n    training content — the baseline \"contamination score\" is uniformly elevated, reducing\n    discriminative power. Without logprob access (prohibitive at 72B scale), S2 Min-K++\n    cannot be computed. S4 Self-Critique is at chance (0.53) because the 72B model''s\n    confidence is well-calibrated from RLHF, obscuring the entropy-based contamination\n    signal.\n\n    Adding 72B to the pool doesn''t meaningfully change any γ estimate (Δγ < 0.01\n    on ARC) because the model sits at θ >> other models, in a region where item response\n    functions are flat (P(correct) ≈ 1 for most items regardless of contamination).\n    The model provides no leverage for estimating γ. Coverage paradoxically worsens\n    slightly (35% → 32%) because the additional model increases posterior concentration\n    without improving calibration of the CIs.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table: 72B accuracy alongside 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B for scale comparison. Shows monotonic\n    increase, 72B near ceiling on ARC/GSM8K.'\n  - 'Table: γ estimates with/without 72B in the pool. Columns: benchmark, γ_without,\n    γ_with, Δγ, CI_width_change. Shows negligible change.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Extended scale plot: |γ| vs log(parameters). Points at 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B/72B. Clear\n    exponential decay toward zero, asymptoting by 7B. 72B indistinguishable from zero.'\n  analysis: 'Capstone for the scale-dependency narrative. At frontier scale: (a) contamination\n    effects are empirically undetectable in accuracy, (b) detection signals degrade\n    below identifiability thresholds, (c) adding a single 72B model to IRT provides\n    essentially zero information gain. The impossibility is triply confirmed: Fisher\n    Information analysis from exp-004 predicts this theoretically, and the 72B observation\n    confirms it empirically.\n\n    For the diagnostic paper: \"Current LLM evaluation operates in a regime where the\n    models most likely contaminated (frontier, trained on massive web corpora) are\n    precisely those where calibration is provably infeasible. The models least likely\n    to need calibration (small, limited pretraining) are the only ones where our methods\n    produce detectable effects — but those effects are confounded with capacity artifacts\n    (Concern #22). This catch-22 is the fundamental structural barrier to post-hoc\n    contamination calibration.\"\n\n    '\n- name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: 'Degraded 2-signal mode using only S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique)\n    — the signals that don''t require model weight access. Tests whether ContamCal\n    is applicable to closed-weight/API-only models. Compares against full pipeline\n    (effectively S1-only given S2/S4 ineffectiveness).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_05B: 0.51\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_15B: 0.53\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_3B: 0.54\n    rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.97\n    auc_gap_mean: 0.44\n    gamma_2signal_arc: -0.06\n    gamma_2signal_arc_ci:\n    - -1.52\n    - 1.4\n    gamma_2signal_arc_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    gamma_2signal_mmlu: 0.11\n    gamma_2signal_mmlu_ci:\n    - -1.38\n    - 1.6\n    gamma_2signal_gsm8k: 0.04\n    gamma_2signal_gsm8k_ci:\n    - -1.25\n    - 1.33\n    gamma_4signal_arc: -1.46\n    gamma_4signal_arc_ci:\n    - -2.51\n    - -0.41\n    calibrated_mae_2signal_arc: 0.085\n    calibrated_mae_4signal_arc: 0.035\n    mae_degradation_factor: 2.4\n    calibrated_mae_2signal_mmlu: 0.092\n    calibrated_mae_2signal_gsm8k: 0.068\n    coverage_95_2signal: 0.18\n    coverage_95_4signal: 0.35\n    ranking_tau_2signal: 0.28\n    ranking_tau_4signal: 0.68\n    linear_regression_mae_2signal: 0.078\n    irt_vs_linear_2signal_p: 0.45\n  result_rationale: 'S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) were both near chance at\n    all scales in exp-008: S2 AUC=0.465 (with directional reversal at d100, Cohen''s\n    d=-1.98), S4 AUC=0.481 at 0.5B. At 1.5B/3B the situation is marginally better\n    but still sub-threshold (S2 ~0.52, S4 ~0.51 extrapolated from signal survival\n    analysis patterns). RRF over two near-chance signals produces composite AUC ≈\n    0.51-0.54, well below the exp-004 identifiability threshold.\n\n    At AUC ≈ 0.52, the exposure scores are essentially random with slight signal.\n    The IRT model receives near-uniform exposure across items and cannot distinguish\n    contaminated from clean items. γ CI spans zero with width >2.8 on all benchmarks\n    — the posterior is dominated by the prior N(0,1). Calibrated MAE is 2.4× worse\n    than 4-signal because the model is fitting noise injected by the random exposure\n    scores. Even linear regression with these garbage exposure features produces MAE=0.078\n    (worse than 4-signal IRT) but better than 2-signal IRT (0.085), confirming that\n    IRT''s complexity is harmful when inputs are noise.\n\n    This proves the \"4-signal pipeline is really a 1-signal pipeline (S1)\" result\n    explicitly: removing S1 collapses the entire framework. S1 requires model weights\n    (embedding extraction). Therefore, ContamCal has zero practical applicability\n    to closed-weight models — the most common deployment mode for frontier models\n    (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini) that are most likely contaminated.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - 'Table: Per-signal AUC by scale showing S2/S4 at chance. Columns: signal, 0.5B,\n    1.5B, 3B. All S2/S4 entries ≈ 0.47-0.54.'\n  - 'Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal pipeline comparison across all metrics. Columns:\n    metric, 2-signal, 4-signal, degradation ratio.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked AUC bar chart: 4-signal (dominated by S1 at 0.97) vs 2-signal (S2+S4\n    at 0.51). Dramatic visual gap. Annotate: ''Without weight access, pipeline operates\n    below identifiability threshold.'''\n  analysis: 'Confirms ContamCal''s dependence on weight access. The method requires\n    embedding- based detection (S1), which requires model weights. The closed-weight\n    models most needing calibration (proprietary frontier models trained on undisclosed\n    data) are precisely those where the method cannot work.\n\n    For the diagnostic paper, this is another impossibility result that strengthens\n    the overall narrative: \"Post-hoc contamination calibration faces a triple impossibility:\n    (1) it requires open-weight access that frontier models don''t provide, (2) it\n    requires N≥50 comparable models that don''t exist for most model families, (3)\n    even when both conditions are met, contamination effects are confounded with model\n    capacity at the scales where calibration would matter most.\"\n\n    The calibration gating criterion catches this: none of the 2-signal benchmarks\n    pass gating (γ not significant, PPP extreme, calibrated MAE > raw MAE). So ContamCal\n    correctly identifies \"don''t calibrate\" — the gating criterion is itself a contribution\n    even when calibration fails.'\n","numLines":634,"startLine":1,"totalLines":634}}}
